<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Lar</h1><p class="st-meta">1150 CE – 1602 CE (approx.) · capital Lar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A long-lived local kingdom in southern Iran on the trade road to Hormuz, whose silver 'larin' coins circulated across the Indian Ocean.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The rulers of Lar, often called the Miladi dynasty, claimed descent from Gurgin son of Milad, a hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a>. They are attested from the 12th century in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laristan">Laristan</a>, the arid region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> and the Gulf, with their capital at Lar.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Lar prospered from its position on the caravan route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> and the port of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ormus">Hormuz</a>, through which Indian, Arabian and Persian goods passed. The kingdom was famous for the larin, a strip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> wire bent into a hook and stamped, which became a common currency in the Gulf, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-maldives">Maldives</a> in the 16th century. Its rulers accepted the overlordship of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> while keeping local autonomy. The population remained largely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1601 to 1602 the Safavid general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alivardi-khan">Allahverdi Khan</a> conquered Lar for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> and deposed its last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-khan-of-lar">Ibrahim Khan</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laristan">Laristan</a> became a Safavid province.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1150 CE – 1602 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.59%;width:9.00%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1150 CE – 1602 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ibrahim Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Larestani, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lar, Miladi dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1376&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lar" aria-label="Kingdom of Lar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6106 1163 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Lar lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6426 1419L6281 1235"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6281 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6426 1419h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6426 1419h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lar. <a href="/atlas?year=1376&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lar">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

